(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-dermatology-pathophys-quiz"]={"title":"Dermatology Pathophysiology Quiz 1 — Clinical Pathophysiology I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which four cell types make up the epidermis, and which predominates?","choices":["Keratinocytes, melanocytes, Merkel cells and Langerhans cells, with keratinocytes about 90 percent","Fibroblasts, macrophages, adipocytes and mast cells, with fibroblasts predominating","Melanocytes, Merkel cells, fibroblasts and histiocytes, with melanocytes predominating","Keratinocytes and melanocytes only, in roughly equal proportion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because keratinocytes dominate so heavily, most epidermal pathology is fundamentally a keratinocyte problem. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A nodule is greater than 5 millimetres. What tissue does it arise from?","choices":["Superficial epidermis only, with no dermal involvement","Fluid accumulating between epidermis and dermis","Dilated superficial dermal capillaries with no inflammatory infiltration at all","Dermal-based tissue with dense collagen bundles, extending into subcutaneous tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Epidermoid cysts are keratin-filled, dermatofibroma is fibrous tissue, and a rheumatoid nodule is fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by histiocytes. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Henoch-Schonlein purpura involves which systems, and which patients?","choices":["The skin alone, with no systemic involvement whatsoever, at any age at all","Skin, joints, kidneys and intestines, commonly in children aged 3 to 10 years","Skin and lungs only, commonly in older adults","Skin and liver, commonly in neonates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — an immunoglobulin A vasculitis, which is why it is not confined to the skin. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How do hypertrophic scars and keloids depart from normal healing?","choices":["Keratinocyte division slows and elastin degrades, thinning the tissue","The inflammatory phase never resolves, so granulation tissue never forms at all and the wound remains open indefinitely without ever progressing to the proliferative or remodelling phases that normally follow","Type I collagen is replaced by weaker type III in basket-weave orientation","Fibroblast dysregulation prolongs the proliferative phase, collagen deposition and degradation become imbalanced during remodelling, and collagen bundles develop haphazardly and exceed the boundaries of the initial wound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two phases go wrong, and exceeding the original wound margin is the defining outcome. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What produces leukonychia, and what else can cause it?","choices":["Overgrowth of connective tissue in the nail bed","Increased capillary density with release of vascular endothelial growth factor into the nail bed tissue itself","Temporary incomplete keratinization at the nail bed, usually harmless nail bed damage but also seen with heavy metal poisoning","A halt of keratin production, associated with illness and malnourishment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — white streaks or spots, also called Mee's lines. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What happens during the inflammatory phase of wound healing, and over what period?","choices":["Days 1 to 3: type I collagen is laid down in basket-weave orientation","Days 1 to 3: a fibrin haemostatic plug forms, neutrophils and macrophages remove dead tissue, and growth factors and cytokines are secreted to signal the next phase","Days 4 to 21: granulation tissue forms, comprised of macrophages, fibroblasts and endothelial cells that rebuild the wound bed from below over three weeks","Days 21 to one year: type III collagen is replaced by type I"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The phase both cleans the wound and issues the signal that starts proliferation. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What distinguishes a vesicle from a bulla, mechanistically as well as by size?","choices":["A vesicle is up to 5 millimetres with fluid containing inflammatory cells collecting within or directly beneath the epidermis; a bulla is greater than 5 millimetres and involves separation of epidermis from dermis","A vesicle is greater than 5 millimetres and a bulla is up to 5 millimetres in diameter, and the two share an identical mechanism of fluid accumulation entirely within the epidermis rather than beneath it","A vesicle is solid and a bulla is fluid-filled","A vesicle contains purulent material and a bulla contains serous fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The bulla represents a deeper plane of cleavage, not merely a bigger blister. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Eczematous dermatitis results from which type of inflammatory reaction?","choices":["T cell-mediated","Immunoglobulin E-mediated mast cell degranulation alone","Neutrophil-driven suppurative inflammation","Complement-mediated vessel destruction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a chronic condition with frequent or infrequent flares, known as the itch that rashes. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which dermal cell mediates immunoglobulin E-mediated inflammation?","choices":["The mast cell","The fibroblast","The melanocyte","The keratinocyte"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and its degranulation is the mechanism behind both the wheal and urticaria. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How are dermatofibroma and lipoma characterised in the tissue?","choices":["Dermatofibroma is a benign enclosed capsule of adipocytes; lipoma is a fibrous tissue overgrowth within the dermis","Both are malignant proliferations of keratinocytes","Dermatofibroma is keratin-filled and lipoma is fluid-filled","Dermatofibroma is a benign overgrowth of fibrous tissue in the dermis; lipoma is a benign enclosed capsule of adipocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both sit under the general term tumor, which describes rapid cellular growth and may or may not be malignant. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What are the two connective tissue layers of the dermis?","choices":["Basale and spinosum","Corneum and lucidum","The papillary and the subcutaneous, being the superficial and the deepest layers","Papillary, the thin superficial layer, and reticular, the dense deeper layer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The dermis is also where the blood vessels and mechanoreceptors sit. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What defines a secondary skin lesion?","choices":["Any lesion that arises from malignant rather than from benign underlying disease processes","A modification or change of a primary lesion caused by infection, trauma or other factors","Any lesion appearing on a second body site after the first","Any lesion that is palpable rather than flat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why a secondary lesion may or may not still resemble what it came from. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What causes petechiae, and what size defines them?","choices":["Mast cell histamine release with plasma leakage, up to 5 millimetres","Minor bleeding into the dermis from capillary rupture, infection, thrombocytopenia or vasculitis, 1 to 2 millimetres or less","Permanent dilatation of the superficial dermal vessels, measuring 1 to 2 millimetres across the surface of the skin","Increased melanin production in the basal layer, up to 5 millimetres"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and they are non-blanchable because the blood sits outside the vessel rather than within it. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which signalling pathway does basal cell carcinoma overactivate, and in which cells?","choices":["The Hedgehog signalling pathway, following ultraviolet-induced mutation of basal keratinocytes in the epidermis","The tp53 tumour suppressor pathway, acting in the differentiated keratinocytes of the epidermis","Fibroblast growth factor receptor-3, in immature keratinocytes","Uninhibited melanocyte growth at the dermal-epidermal junction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the most common skin cancer and the most common malignancy in humans, slow growing and rarely metastasising. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What is the pathophysiology of actinic keratosis, and what does it carry a higher risk of?","choices":["Benign proliferation of immature keratinocytes, carrying a higher risk for basal cell carcinoma of the head and neck region in later life especially","Human papillomavirus infection of basal cells, carrying higher risk for melanoma","Autoimmune T cell infiltration, carrying higher risk for lymphoma","Damage from excessive cumulative ultraviolet exposure leading to intraepidermal proliferation of dysplastic keratinocytes, carrying higher risk for squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is described as the most common precancer, which is exactly why the dysplasia matters. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"How do petechiae, purpura and ecchymoses differ?","choices":["By size — petechiae 1 to 2 millimetres or less, purpura 3 to 10 millimetres, ecchymoses greater than 10 millimetres — with similar underlying pathology","By mechanism — petechiae from capillary rupture, purpura from vessel dilatation, and ecchymoses from melanin deposition in the dermis rather than blood","By depth — petechiae epidermal, purpura dermal, ecchymoses subcutaneous","By blanchability — petechiae blanch, purpura and ecchymoses do not"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a single process of bleeding into the dermis described at three scales. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What distinguishes a primary from a secondary skin lesion?","choices":["A primary lesion is the one that appears first in time, and a secondary lesion is any lesion that appears later at a different site elsewhere on the body","A primary lesion is the direct result of the underlying disease and retains its original unmodified appearance; a secondary lesion has been modified over time","A primary lesion is palpable and a secondary lesion is flat","A primary lesion is benign and a secondary lesion is malignant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and a secondary lesion may or may not still resemble the lesion it came from. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is a fissure, and what conditions produce one?","choices":["A thickened plaque produced by chronic rubbing","A dilated superficial vessel without inflammatory infiltrate","A linear ulcer forming a crack in the epidermis, from loss of elasticity or flexibility, severe dryness and mechanical tension, with hyperkeratosis thickening the stratum corneum","A superficial loss of the epidermis that spares the dermal-epidermal junction and everything beneath it, so no collagen is ever destroyed in the process of healing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is a linear form of ulceration rather than a distinct process. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which tinea subtype affects the nails, and what is its other name?","choices":["Tinea barbae, affecting nails and beard","Tinea unguium, also called onychomycosis","Tinea capitis, also called onychomycosis","Tinea manuum, affecting the nails of the hand"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Corporis is trunk and limbs, pedis foot, cruris groin, capitis head, facialis face, manuum hand, barbae beard. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What tissue makes up a scale?","choices":["Focal loss of epidermis and dermis with destruction of the collagen beneath","A compact portion of desquamating stratum corneum, varying in size and thickness","Dried sebum, cellular debris, blood or necrotic skin","Thickened epidermis induced by long-term scratching"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and psoriasis is the example given — which follows from its epidermal hyperplasia and loss of the granular layer. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Where does each step of vitamin D activation take place, beginning in the skin?","choices":["Cholecalciferol is produced in the skin under ultraviolet B light, then hydroxylated first in the liver and then in the kidneys to the active form calcitriol","Calcitriol is produced directly in the skin under ultraviolet B light and requires no further modification in either the liver or the kidneys at all","Cholecalciferol is produced in the liver, then hydroxylated in the skin and finally in the kidneys","Cholecalciferol is produced in the skin and hydroxylated entirely within the kidneys in a single step"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The skin only starts the process; two further hydroxylation steps in separate organs are needed before the hormone is active. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which structural epidermal changes occur in psoriasis?","choices":["Separation of the epidermis from the dermis with fluid accumulating between the two layers of the skin itself","Loss of melanocytes from the basal layer","Epidermal hyperplasia, loss of the stratum granulosum, and failure of epidermal cells to secrete lipids producing xeroderma","Thinning of the epidermis with slowed keratinocyte division"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Losing the granular layer is what leaves the characteristic scale. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What defines telangiectasia at the tissue level?","choices":["Dense inflammatory infiltration around dilated vessels","Proliferation of new vessels driven by release of vascular endothelial growth factor into the surrounding dermis over a prolonged period","Permanent dilatation and thinning of the endothelium of capillaries, venules and arterioles in the superficial dermis, with NO inflammatory cell infiltration","Capillary rupture with blood deposited into the dermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The absence of inflammatory infiltrate is the discriminating feature. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is sebum primarily composed of?","choices":["Water, sodium and chloride electrolytes","Keratin and keratohyalin","Collagen and elastin","Triglycerides, wax esters and squalene"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, produced by the sebaceous glands associated with hair follicles to lubricate skin and hair. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which layer of the epidermis is the site of cellular division?","choices":["The granular layer, known as the stratum granulosum","The stratum lucidum","The basal layer, or stratum basale, the deepest layer","The stratum corneum, the outermost layer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A single layer of rapidly dividing columnar keratinocytes, which also contains melanocytes and Merkel cells. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What happens to collagen during the remodelling phase, and how does the result differ from normal dermis?","choices":["Type III collagen is replaced with stronger type I collagen oriented in small parallel bundles, whereas normal dermis has a basket-weave orientation","Type I collagen is replaced with weaker type III collagen oriented in a basket-weave pattern throughout the scar tissue that results","Collagen is degraded entirely and replaced by elastin","Collagen production continues to increase indefinitely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The scar is strong but architecturally different, which is why it never quite matches the surrounding skin. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which hair type is regulated by androgens, and which grows independently of them?","choices":["All three types are equally androgen-dependent","Terminal hair is androgen-regulated; vellus hair grows independently of androgens","Vellus hair is regulated by androgens; terminal hair grows independently of them","Lanugo hair is androgen-regulated; terminal hair is not"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lanugo is the third type, the fine hair of newborns. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How does human papillomavirus produce a common wart?","choices":["It triggers mast cell degranulation in the dermis","It digests keratin using keratinases and proteases","It invades epidermal basal cells through microabrasions, leading to proliferation of the epidermis","It infects the melanocytes that lie along the dermal-epidermal junction within the epidermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and clinical variants of warts are often associated with distinct viral subtypes. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which gene mutation is named for squamous cell carcinoma, and which histological features are pathognomonic?","choices":["Overactivation of the Hedgehog pathway, with pearly papules and telangiectasias","Activating mutation of fibroblast growth factor receptor-3, with a stuck-on waxy and scaly appearance on the skin","Autosomal dominant inheritance with variable penetrance and radial growth","Mutation in the tp53 tumour suppressor gene, with keratin pearls and epithelial pearls as pathognomonic features"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It derives from keratinocytes, and immunosuppression is a notable risk factor. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Why does psoriatic skin become dry, in the lecture's account?","choices":["The stratum corneum is lost entirely","Sebaceous glands overproduce sebum, which dries the surface","Epidermal cells fail to secrete lipids, producing xeroderma","Eccrine sweat glands are destroyed by inflammation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the dryness follows from a secretory failure rather than from water loss alone. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 62"}]};
